# G019 — Check if Palindrome

*v2. See `palindrome.md` for prose: palindrome as composition of
reverse + equality, normalisation as projection (the `where` in
disguise), longest-palindrome as content search, expand-around-centre
as local-to-global pattern.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Quality Verification Gate

[quality-verify @content
    IN ORDER TO
        gate submission behind structural property checks
        where normalised palindrome detection is one of several verifiers
        composed from reverse + equality on a normalised projection

    <- [palindrome-normalized? {s: @content}]
        where true -> #easter-egg-bonus

    @length <- [length {s: @content}]
    @length
        where at most @max-chars -> #length-ok
        otherwise                 -> #too-long

    @longest <- [longest-palindrome {s: @content}]
    @longest
        where length at least 5 -> #contains-palindrome-gem
]

---

## Verb Surface

[palindrome?
    {s}                                      ;; exact (case + punctuation sensitive)
]

[palindrome-normalized?
    {s}                                       ;; case-folded, punctuation-stripped
]

[longest-palindrome
    {s}                                       ;; -> longest palindromic substring
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: palindrome composes `where` reverse = identity

[palindrome? {s: @s}]
    IN ORDER TO
        name a concept the resolver could derive but deserves a name
        because symmetry detection recurs across domains

    [reverse-string {s: @s}] is equal to @s
]

The resolver could always rewrite `@palindrome?` as reverse + `==`.
It commits to the named form because the concept is load-bearing —
symmetry detection generalises to grammar checking, format
validation, plagiarism scoring.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: normalisation as `where` projection defines the frame

[palindrome-normalized? {s: @s}]
    @projected <- @s
        lowercased
        with non-alphanumeric stripped
    [palindrome? {s: @projected}]

    IN ORDER TO
        ignore details that don't matter for the question
        because every `where` clause is a normalisation —
        it declares which details count and which are noise
]

"Race car" is not a palindrome. "racecar" is. The difference is
what you choose to ignore. G019 makes that choice explicit at the
verb level.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: expand-around-centre as `where` hypothesis at each position

[longest-palindrome {s: @s}]
    for each @i in positions of @s
        expand around @i (odd-length candidate)
        expand around (@i, @i+1) (even-length candidate)
        record the longest palindromic substring found
    return best over all positions
    IN ORDER TO
        test each position as a potential centre of symmetry
        and keep the winner — the local-to-global pattern the
        resolver will see again in graph search and tree matching
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [palindrome? {s: "racecar"}]                          ;; -> true
    -> [palindrome? {s: "Race car"}]                         ;; -> false

    -> [palindrome-normalized? {s: "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"}]
    ;; -> true (normalised)

    -> [longest-palindrome {s: "forgeeksskeegfor"}]          ;; -> "geeksskeeg"
]
